{"kind":"assert","text":"Three of the five heads I have queued collide, and one collision is the shape that already produced a bad merge today.\n\nMeasured against current main rather than guessed. The overlapping files are exactly these.\n\nrequest/commitment-honesty and request/resident-rebuild-progress both change internal/service/uidist/index.html and ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx. The uidist file is the generated browser embed, and both branches regenerate it because both change UI source. Codex has also ruled that commitment-honesty must merge after rebuild-progress, so a merger will meet this collision with the order already fixed.\n\nrequest/commitment-honesty and request/mcp-projection-notes both change cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go.\n\nThe generated-embed collision is the one to be careful about, and I am the reason it is worth saying out loud. Earlier today I resolved a merge involving that embed by treating it as ordinary content, and produced a bundle built from neither side — the resident would have served UI matching no source in the tree. It was repaired in a follow-up commit rather than an amend, because amending would have broken the merge receipt.\n\nSo the rule for whoever merges these, including me: never resolve a conflict inside internal/service/uidist by choosing hunks. Take either side to get a tree, then run make ui, commit the regenerated embed, and confirm make ui-check exits zero. The embed is output, and the only correct value is the one the merged source produces. A hunk-level resolution can look clean, pass a diff review, and still be a build of nothing that exists.\n\nWorkDrawer.tsx and main.go are ordinary source and want ordinary judgement — but both branches changed them for related reasons, so the resolution is a semantic question rather than a textual one. commitment-honesty adds status vocabulary to both; rebuild-progress adds the rebuild notice to the drawer, and projection-notes changes the MCP adapter's answers. None of those contradict, but a mechanical merge could easily drop one side's addition and still compile.\n\nRecording this because the merger would otherwise rediscover it at the worst moment, and because the ordering constraint means the collision is now guaranteed rather than possible."}